## Summary
- Use own-key checks when selecting installed plugin and hook-pack update targets from CLI input.
- Preserve npm package matching for prototype-named package names such as constructor.
- Add focused regression coverage for plugin and hook update selection.

## What Problem This Solves
The plugin and hook update selectors used rawId in installs to decide whether CLI input named an installed entry. Because install maps are normal objects, inherited Object prototype keys such as constructor were treated as installed ids even when no own install record existed. That can make plugin update skip npm-package matching for a real tracked package, and can make hook-pack update try to update a non-existent hook id.

## Evidence
Real environment tested: Local OpenClaw checkout on Linux, Node.js v22.22.0, using the real exported resolvePluginUpdateSelection and resolveHookPackUpdateSelection functions.

Before fix on current main, this input used an installed npm plugin package whose resolved name is constructor but whose tracked install id is tracked-constructor. It also checked an empty hook install map with rawId constructor:
~~~js
const pluginInstall = { source: 'npm', spec: 'constructor', resolvedName: 'constructor', installPath: '/tmp/plugin' };
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  plugin: resolvePluginUpdateSelection({ installs: { 'tracked-constructor': pluginInstall }, rawId: 'constructor' }),
  hook: resolveHookPackUpdateSelection({ installs: {}, rawId: 'constructor' }),
}, null, 2));
~~~

Current main misclassified the inherited constructor key as an installed id:
~~~text
{
  "plugin": {
    "pluginIds": [
      "constructor"
    ]
  },
  "hook": {
    "hookIds": [
      "constructor"
    ]
  }
}
~~~

After fix on this branch, the same real selector calls use only own install records:
~~~text
{
  "plugin": {
    "pluginIds": [
      "tracked-constructor"
    ],
    "specOverrides": {
      "tracked-constructor": "constructor"
    }
  },
  "hook": {
    "hookIds": []
  }
}
~~~

## Verification
- node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/cli/plugins-update-selection.test.ts -- --reporter=verbose - passed, 1 file / 8 tests.
- ./node_modules/.bin/oxlint --tsconfig config/tsconfig/oxlint.core.json src/cli/plugins-update-selection.ts src/cli/plugins-update-selection.test.ts - passed.
- git diff --check - passed.
- Collision check: open PR changed-file search found no open PR modifying src/cli/plugins-update-selection.ts or src/cli/plugins-update-selection.test.ts before opening.

## Summary
- Clear the zalouser probe timeout handle once the auth lookup resolves or rejects.
- Keep the existing timeout fallback behavior for slow auth lookups.
- Collision check: open PR file search returned no PR modifying extensions/zalouser/src/probe.ts before opening.

## What Problem This Solves
Zalouser probe calls with a timeout scheduled a fallback timer but did not clear that timer when the auth lookup completed first. A normal fast local lookup could return while one stale fallback timer remained pending.

## Evidence
Real environment tested: Local OpenClaw checkout on Linux, Node.js v22.22.0. The comparison uses the real probeZalouser implementation and real getZaloUserInfo(profile) local session lookup path with a disposable profile name that has no saved Zalo session. No external Zalo account, phone number, endpoint, token, or credential is used.

Exact steps or command run after this patch:
~~~bash
node --import tsx --input-type=module <<'EOF_SCRIPT'
import { probeZalouser } from './extensions/zalouser/src/probe.ts';

const realSetTimeout = globalThis.setTimeout;
const realClearTimeout = globalThis.clearTimeout;
const active = new Set();

globalThis.setTimeout = ((fn, ms, ...args) => {
  const handle = realSetTimeout(fn, ms, ...args);
  active.add(handle);
  console.log('[timer] scheduled ' + ms);
  return handle;
});

globalThis.clearTimeout = ((handle) => {
  const had = active.delete(handle);
  console.log('[timer] cleared had=' + had);
  return realClearTimeout(handle);
});

const result = await probeZalouser('proof-no-session', 10);
console.log(JSON.stringify({ result, activeProbeTimersAfterReturn: active.size }));
for (const handle of active) realClearTimeout(handle);
globalThis.setTimeout = realSetTimeout;
globalThis.clearTimeout = realClearTimeout;
EOF_SCRIPT
~~~

Evidence before fix on current main, commit 65805e519d, with upstream/main still having the same extensions/zalouser/src/probe.ts timeout race shape:
~~~text
[timer] scheduled 1000
{"result":{"ok":false,"error":"No saved Zalo session for profile \"proof-no-session\""},"activeProbeTimersAfterReturn":1}
~~~

Evidence after fix on this PR branch, commit db7e5b74df:
~~~text
[timer] scheduled 1000
[timer] cleared had=true
{"result":{"ok":false,"error":"No saved Zalo session for profile \"proof-no-session\""},"activeProbeTimersAfterReturn":0}
~~~

Observed result after fix: The real Zalouser probe lookup path still reports the expected no-session error for a disposable profile, but the fallback timeout handle is cleared before probeZalouser returns. Current main returns with one fallback probe timer still pending.

What was not tested: No external Zalo account login was exercised. This is a local non-mocked runtime transcript for the real local session lookup path, not a full live authenticated Zalouser account proof.

## Verification
- node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.extension-zalo.config.ts extensions/zalouser/src/probe.test.ts -t "clears the probe timeout after auth resolves" --reporter verbose - passed, 1 focused spec.
- node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/zalouser/src/probe.test.ts - passed, 1 file / 6 specs.
- Non-mocked runtime comparison above: current main leaves one fallback probe timer pending after the real local session lookup returns; this branch clears it before returning.
- git diff --check - passed.

## Summary
- Use own-key checks when flattening MCP loopback union schema properties.
- Keep required fields only when the flattened schema defines an own property for that key.
- Add focused regression coverage for Object prototype property names such as toString.

## What Problem This Solves
MCP loopback tools can expose JSON schema properties with names like toString or constructor. Current main uses key in mergedProps while flattening anyOf/oneOf schemas, so Object.prototype keys are treated as already present even when the flattened schema has no own property. That can drop the actual property schema while still leaving the key in required.

## Evidence
Real environment tested: Local OpenClaw checkout on Linux, Node.js v22.22.0, using a real buildMcpToolSchema invocation against a loopback tool schema.

Before fix on current main, this input:
~~~js
buildMcpToolSchema([{
  name: 'proof_tool',
  description: 'proof',
  parameters: {
    anyOf: [{
      type: 'object',
      properties: { toString: { type: 'string' } },
      required: ['toString'],
    }],
  },
}]);
~~~

produced a warning and omitted the own toString property:
~~~text
mcp loopback: conflicting schema definitions for "toString", keeping the first variant
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {},
  "required": [
    "toString"
  ]
}
own toString: false
value type: function
~~~

After fix on this branch, the same real schema projection keeps the property as an own key:
~~~text
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "toString": {
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "toString"
  ]
}
own toString: true
value: {"type":"string"}
~~~

The focused regression also covers the inverse case where a required field names toString but no variant defines that property. The fixed code no longer keeps that inherited prototype name in required.

## Verification
- node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/mcp-http.schema.test.ts -- --reporter=verbose - passed, 4 gateway projects / 8 tests.
- ./node_modules/.bin/oxlint --tsconfig config/tsconfig/oxlint.core.json src/gateway/mcp-http.schema.ts src/gateway/mcp-http.schema.test.ts - passed.
- git diff --check - passed.
- Collision note: #75141 also touches src/gateway/mcp-http.schema.ts and src/gateway/mcp-http.schema.test.ts, but it is open, conflicting, proof-blocked, and ClawSweeper marked it superseded. This PR is the narrow own-key fix and keeps the warning-policy behavior separate.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users resolving exec-backed SecretRefs could get inherited Object prototype members treated as provider response entries when the requested id matched names such as `toString`.

## Why This Change Was Made

Exec provider responses now check `values` and `errors` with own-property membership before accepting an id. The change is scoped to exec response parsing and keeps the existing missing-id and per-id error behavior.

## User Impact

Users and operators get deterministic SecretRef failures when an exec provider omits a requested id, including ids that collide with Object prototype names, instead of accidentally reading inherited values.

## Real Behavior Proof

Behavior addressed: an exec SecretRef provider can omit a requested id named `toString`; the resolver must treat that as a missing provider response entry, not as an inherited Object prototype member.

Environment: local checkout of this PR branch `alix/secrets-exec-own-ids`, Node.js via the repository `tsx` loader. The provider was a real executable shell script created in a private temp directory with mode `0700`; it read stdin and returned the real exec-provider protocol payload `{"protocolVersion":1,"values":{}}` on stdout.

Command run after this patch:

```bash
node --import tsx --input-type=module <<'EOF'
import fs from "node:fs/promises";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { resolveSecretRefString } from "./src/secrets/resolve.ts";

const root = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "openclaw-exec-secretref-proof-"));
await fs.chmod(root, 0o700);
const provider = path.join(root, "missing-toString-provider.sh");
await fs.writeFile(
  provider,
  [
    "#!/bin/sh",
    "cat >/dev/null",
    "printf '%s' '{\"protocolVersion\":1,\"values\":{}}'",
  ].join("\n"),
  { mode: 0o700 },
);
await fs.chmod(provider, 0o700);

const config = {
  secrets: {
    providers: {
      liveexec: {
        source: "exec",
        command: provider,
        passEnv: ["PATH"],
      },
    },
  },
};

const ref = { source: "exec", provider: "liveexec", id: "toString" };
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  proof: "exec SecretRef provider missing inherited id",
  providerScript: provider.replace(root, "<tmp-proof-dir>"),
  providerOutput: { protocolVersion: 1, values: {} },
  requestedRef: ref,
}, null, 2));

try {
  await resolveSecretRefString(ref, { config, env: { PATH: process.env.PATH ?? "" } });
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ result: "unexpected-success" }, null, 2));
  process.exitCode = 1;
} catch (error) {
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    result: "expected-failure",
    errorName: error?.name,
    errorMessage: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),
  }, null, 2));
  if (!(error instanceof Error) || !error.message.includes('response missing id "toString"')) {
    process.exitCode = 1;
  }
}

await fs.rm(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
EOF
```

Observed output:

```json
{
  "proof": "exec SecretRef provider missing inherited id",
  "providerScript": "<tmp-proof-dir>/missing-toString-provider.sh",
  "providerOutput": {
    "protocolVersion": 1,
    "values": {}
  },
  "requestedRef": {
    "source": "exec",
    "provider": "liveexec",
    "id": "toString"
  }
}
{
  "result": "expected-failure",
  "errorName": "SecretRefResolutionError",
  "errorMessage": "Exec provider \"liveexec\" response missing id \"toString\"."
}
```

This exercises the real exec-provider path without mocking `spawn`: OpenClaw starts the configured provider command, sends the protocol request over stdin, parses stdout, then resolves the requested `exec:liveexec:toString` SecretRef. After the patch, the missing `toString` id fails deterministically as a missing response id.

What was not tested: no external secret manager or private credential store was contacted; the provider script returned only an empty protocol response and no secret material.

## Evidence

- `git diff --check`
- `node scripts/test-projects.mjs src/secrets/resolve.test.ts` (27 tests passed)
- Live exec SecretRef proof above: real executable provider returning an empty response for requested id `toString` now fails with `Exec provider "liveexec" response missing id "toString".`
- Pre-PR collision check: no open PRs touched `src/secrets/resolve.ts` or `src/secrets/resolve.test.ts`; no open PR covered exec response id inherited-key handling.

## CI Note

The only observed CI failure at the time of this update was `checks-node-compact-small-whole-2`, timing out in `src/tui/tui-pty-local.e2e.test.ts > creates and adopts a fresh session through the real Gateway backend`. That shard does not touch `src/secrets/resolve.ts` or `src/secrets/resolve.test.ts`; the focused secrets test and the full secrets project test passed locally.

## Summary

- add dynamic configured-model choices to the native `/model` command
- enable the existing command argument menu path for `/model` so Slack can render model picks with its existing buttons/static_select/external_select machinery
- keep bare `/model` status behavior when no configured model choices are available

## Details

`/think` already gets rich Slack command UI because its registry entry declares choices plus an argument menu. `/model` had a positional argument but no choices/menu metadata, so Slack had nothing to render before dispatching the command as plain text.

This change keeps the fix in the shared command registry: configured catalog entries become `provider/model` choices, and the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter handles the selected value. No Slack-specific command renderer changes are needed.

## Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/commands-registry.test.ts`
- `git diff --check HEAD~1..HEAD`
- `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` registry smoke shown below

## Real behavior proof

Behavior addressed: Slack native slash-command UX for `/model` can now surface the existing command argument menu instead of requiring users to type a model id manually.

Real environment tested: local OpenClaw source checkout in an isolated worktree from `origin/main`, running the actual command registry module after this patch.

Exact steps or command run after this patch: ran this local terminal command against the patched checkout:

```bash
node --import tsx - <<'EOF'
import { findCommandByNativeName, resolveCommandArgMenu } from './src/auto-reply/commands-registry.ts';
const command = findCommandByNativeName('model');
if (!command) throw new Error('missing /model command');
const menu = resolveCommandArgMenu({
  command,
  cfg: {
    models: {
      providers: {
        openai: { models: [{ id: 'gpt-5.5', name: 'GPT-5.5' }] },
        anthropic: { models: [{ id: 'claude-sonnet-4.6', name: 'Claude Sonnet 4.6' }] },
      },
    },
  },
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ arg: menu?.arg.name, choices: menu?.choices }, null, 2));
EOF
```

Evidence after fix: terminal output from the patched OpenClaw registry smoke:

```json
{
  "arg": "model",
  "choices": [
    {
      "value": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
      "label": "anthropic/Claude Sonnet 4.6"
    },
    {
      "value": "openai/gpt-5.5",
      "label": "openai/GPT-5.5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Observed result after fix: `/model` now resolves a native command argument menu for configured model choices, using `provider/model` values that the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter dispatch path already accepts. Focused registry tests also passed 31/31.

What was not tested: live Slack Block Kit interaction against a real Slack workspace. The Slack plugin path reused here is already covered by existing arg-menu render/dispatch tests; this PR adds the missing registry metadata that feeds that path.

## Notes

AI-assisted with human-requested agent workflow. The local commit helper hydrated ignored `node_modules` in the isolated worktree before committing; no tracked dependency or lockfile changes are included.

